 dict(pmid='43063888', doi='10.1016/j.wasman.2026.115996', journal='Waste Manag', title='Particle removal by wet spraying scrubbers: an experimental study in a municipal waste incineration plant.', tier='B', basis='Lab / research instrument', short='Downscaled wet spray scrubber on incinerator flue gas: >90% removal of 0.3-7 um particles with 97 um droplets and 10 L/m3 liquid-to-gas; contact time 4-10 s irrelevant', design='Engineering control device test', pm='Inlet and outlet particle mass and number size distributions (mode ~0.3 um), metal and carbon content, oxidative potential, SEM morphology', geo='France', endpoint='Fractional particle removal efficiency of a wet spraying scrubber', affil='not stated; not stated', n='A downscaled wet spraying scrubber fed with a slipstream of municipal waste incinerator flue gas was run under nine operating conditions, with particle mass and number measured upstream and downstream and fractional efficiencies computed from number size distributions. Most particles were submicron (mode ~0.3 um) with carbon below 1 wt% and Na and K near 10 wt% each; larger particles carried more metal and higher oxidative potential. Removal exceeded 90% for 0.3-7 um particles with small droplets (nominal D32 97 um) and a high liquid-to-gas ratio (10 L/m3), while gas-spray contact time between 4 and 10 s had no observed effect. Weakness: a downscaled unit on a flue-gas fraction, nine conditions without replication statistics, and no CIs. Relevance: submicron modes with composition-dependent oxidative potential are the fraction optical sensors size worst.', sub=corpus.OCC),
